Layer types: {{ layer_types() }}.

{{ rules('bar') }}
- A bar `x` reads up to 50 values: draw 120 months as a `line`, or roll them up in SQL.

Derived series (rolling averages, running totals, YoY) are window functions or extra columns, plotted as another `y` or a `layers:` entry. The delivered page is static: never caption anything as available "on hover".

`annotations:` (bar, line, area, scatter) are `type: rule` (a line at `y: 25`, `y: avg()`, `x: <date>`), `band` (`from`/`to`), `note` (at `x` and `y`) or `trend` (`fit: linear()`), each with `text`. One target value is a rule; a target per bar or period is the targets recipe. Peak note: `y: max()`, no `x`; never paste a result.

{{ recipe_names() }}, targets and pacers are recipes, never a `type:`: read them on {{ s('page_recipes') }} first.

## Variables

A variable is an input on the page (a dropdown, slider or date picker) whose value a query reads by bare name: `{{ '{{ region }}' }}`, never `variables.region`. Lead with `column: table.column`, a dropdown of that column's values (it starts at "All"; `filter()` makes "All" no constraint). Omit `default`. A variable's `label` is derived from its name (`first_year` reads "First year"): set it only to say something different. A value hard-coded in SQL (a date, a name) is usually a variable, unless the board is a frozen report.

A range bound is two variables on one column (`column: cars.model_year`), used as `filter('model_year', first_year, '>=')` and `'<='`; unset ("All") means no bound, so no `default`, `data_type`, `input` or static list.

A date range is one variable, not two `input: date` variables with hard-coded defaults: `period: {column: t.order_date, input: daterange}` read as `{{ "{{ filter_date_range('order_date', period) }}" }}`. Unset means all dates; a rolling window is a preset name, which `default:` and a SQL literal (`filter_date_range('order_ts', 'last_30_days')`) both take. Names: `today`, `yesterday`, `last_<N>_<unit>`, `previous_<N>_<unit>`, `this_<unit>`, `<unit>_to_date`, with units days/weeks/months/quarters/years and `fiscal_` before quarter/year (`previous_3_quarters`, `this_fiscal_quarter`). `presets:` lists the picker's menu.

